Not really recommended for reading
A while ago that I read, just happened to see some scribbled notes of reading thoughts on my memory.
This book is more of a collection of fragments about love from life, rather than a short story collection.
The tone of this book is extremely bland, like plain water that is bland, pursuing simplicity to an extreme in its choice of words and sentences, and the plot is also as quiet and bland as a street on a summer afternoon.
Every short story is a snapshot of a small moment in ordinary life, with no beginning and no end.
Finally gathered together, entitled "When We Talk About Love, What Are We Talking About?"
I think that this collection of novels, as well as its title, is like an art of behaviour, and the author wants to express that love is more than just mourning on the stage, the sea of ruins in urban legends, not only that you make me feel like paint, love is more than that and shouldn't be represented above, love is also life as flat as water, as dry as day to day, as well as the restlessness of life, the escape from reality, the uncertainty of the future.
Reading this book, you may recall couples arguing beside you, boring marriages of people you know, and unhappy relationships.
This is precisely why I do not recommend this book: from an aesthetic standpoint, the style and plot of the novel are unremarkable; unless your life is like raging waters or as sweet as living in a candy castle, you will find yourself feeling bored when you read it.
And in terms of depth of thought, everyday situations do not surprise or expand one’s mind and perspective (this is unlike in Gabriel Garcia Marquez’s Love in the Time of Cholera), while brief episodes fail to address any issues, such as what kind of mistake led to the failure of love?
What causes the breakdown of the protagonist's marriage?
So I wouldn't recommend reading this book; it doesn't really matter whether one reads it or not.
The author’s ideas are conveyed more through technique and genre than through the narrative plot, which is also a reason for the poor reading experience of this book.
